---
import { Code } from "astro:components"

import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro"
import Card from "../components/Card.astro"

import defaultConfig from "../../../default.lua?raw"
---

<Layout title="Home">
  <section>
    <h1>Overview</h1>
    <p>
      <i>boring</i> is a minimalist, non-monolithic Wayland compositor built on
      <a href="https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/wlroots/wlroots">wlroots</a> that is
      designed to be hackable and extensible. Functionality is provided through plugins;
      we give you the tools and leave you to play.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unlike most compositors, <i>boring</i> implements only the bare necessities
      of a compositor. This is what is meant by <i>non-monolithic</i>. Window
      management, decorations, and desktop components are provided through
      plugins loadable at runtime instead of the compositor itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      This doesn't mean that <i>boring</i> is featureless or incomplete. An <a
        href="https://github.com/loukamb/boring/tree/trunk/plugins"
        >extensive collection of default plugins</a
      > is available to provide you with most of the functionality you should need.
      However, if you are not satisfied with how the default plugins work, you can
      very easily modify them or create your own altogether, a luxury you rarely get
      with other compositors.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h1>Implementation</h1>
    <p>
      <i>boring</i> is written in Lua. It specifically makes use of <a
        href="https://luajit.org/luajit.html">LuaJIT</a
      > for its performance and its binding capabilities. No native code is used at
      any point, other than in the form of the libraries the compositor depends on.
      Plugins and configuration files are also written in Lua; they have access to
      the whole of the compositor's API, so plugins shouldn't be too limited.
    </p>
    <p>
      We try to support as many Wayland protocols as possible. We make use of
      wlroots to help us, but protocols unsupported by wlroots will be provided
      by the compositor itself. You can list which protocols we report for by
      invoking the compositor with the <code>--protocols</code> flag.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h1>Configuration</h1>
    <p>
      Configuration files in <i>boring</i> are essentially headless plugins. They
      have the same environment as plugins, but unlike plugins, they are reloaded
      automatically when changes are detected and cannot be unmounted without physically
      removing the files from the filesystem.
    </p>
    <p>
      The configuration file is located at <code
        >~/.config/boring/config.lua</code
      >. You can write additional configuration files and include them in your
      main configuration file using <code>require</code>. If the configuration
      file is missing, the default configuration below will be used.
    </p>
    <h2>Default configuration</h2>
    <Code code={defaultConfig} lang="lua" theme="github-light-high-contrast" />
  </section>
</Layout>
